@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Error Handling";
}

<div class="container-fluid">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">

        <h1 class="display-5 fw-bold">
            Error Handling in SQL Server
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            Learn how to detect, handle and manage errors in SQL Server
            using TRY...CATCH, error functions, THROW and RAISERROR.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         1. WHAT IS ERROR HANDLING?
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                1. What is Error Handling?
            </h2>

            <p>
                Error Handling is the process of detecting and managing
                errors that occur during the execution of SQL statements.
            </p>

            <p>
                Without proper error handling, an unexpected error can
                stop the execution of a SQL batch, stored procedure or
                transaction.
            </p>

            <p>
                SQL Server provides the
                <strong>TRY...CATCH</strong> mechanism to handle
                runtime errors.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         2. WHY ERROR HANDLING?
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                2. Why is Error Handling Important?
            </h2>

            <ul>

                <li>
                    It prevents unexpected termination of SQL operations.
                </li>

                <li>
                    It provides useful information about errors.
                </li>

                <li>
                    It allows applications to respond properly to errors.
                </li>

                <li>
                    It helps maintain database consistency.
                </li>

                <li>
                    It is especially important when working with transactions.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         3. TRY...CATCH
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                3. TRY...CATCH
            </h2>

            <p>
                SQL Server uses the <strong>TRY...CATCH</strong> construct
                to handle runtime errors.
            </p>

            <p>
                Statements that may generate an error are placed inside
                the <strong>TRY</strong> block.
            </p>

            <p>
                If an error occurs, SQL Server transfers control to the
                <strong>CATCH</strong> block.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRY

    -- SQL statements

END TRY
BEGIN CATCH

    -- Error handling statements

END CATCH;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         4. SIMPLE TRY CATCH EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                4. Simple TRY...CATCH Example
            </h2>

            <p>
                The following example generates a divide-by-zero error.
                The error is handled by the CATCH block.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRY

    SELECT 10 / 0 AS Result;

END TRY
BEGIN CATCH

    PRINT 'An error occurred.';

END CATCH;</code></pre>

            <div class="alert alert-info mt-3 mb-0">

                <strong>Explanation:</strong>

                <ul class="mb-0 mt-2">

                    <li>
                        <strong>TRY</strong> contains the statement that
                        may generate an error.
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <strong>10 / 0</strong> generates a divide-by-zero error.
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        Control moves to the <strong>CATCH</strong> block.
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        The error message is printed.
                    </li>

                </ul>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         5. ERROR_NUMBER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                5. ERROR_NUMBER()
            </h2>

            <p>
                <strong>ERROR_NUMBER()</strong> returns the error number
                generated by SQL Server.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRY

    SELECT 10 / 0;

END TRY
BEGIN CATCH

    SELECT ERROR_NUMBER() AS ErrorNumber;

END CATCH;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         6. ERROR_MESSAGE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                6. ERROR_MESSAGE()
            </h2>

            <p>
                <strong>ERROR_MESSAGE()</strong> returns the complete
                error message generated by SQL Server.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRY

    SELECT 10 / 0;

END TRY
BEGIN CATCH

    SELECT ERROR_MESSAGE() AS ErrorMessage;

END CATCH;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         7. ERROR_SEVERITY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                7. ERROR_SEVERITY()
            </h2>

            <p>
                <strong>ERROR_SEVERITY()</strong> returns the severity
                level of the error.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRY

    SELECT 10 / 0;

END TRY
BEGIN CATCH

    SELECT ERROR_SEVERITY() AS ErrorSeverity;

END CATCH;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         8. ERROR_STATE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                8. ERROR_STATE()
            </h2>

            <p>
                <strong>ERROR_STATE()</strong> returns the state number
                associated with the error.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRY

    SELECT 10 / 0;

END TRY
BEGIN CATCH

    SELECT ERROR_STATE() AS ErrorState;

END CATCH;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         9. ERROR_LINE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                9. ERROR_LINE()
            </h2>

            <p>
                <strong>ERROR_LINE()</strong> returns the line number
                at which the error occurred.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRY

    SELECT 10 / 0;

END TRY
BEGIN CATCH

    SELECT ERROR_LINE() AS ErrorLine;

END CATCH;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         10. ERROR_PROCEDURE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                10. ERROR_PROCEDURE()
            </h2>

            <p>
                <strong>ERROR_PROCEDURE()</strong> returns the name of
                the stored procedure or trigger where the error occurred.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRY

    SELECT 10 / 0;

END TRY
BEGIN CATCH

    SELECT ERROR_PROCEDURE() AS ErrorProcedure;

END CATCH;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         11. ALL ERROR INFORMATION
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                11. Retrieve Complete Error Information
            </h2>

            <p>
                We can use multiple error functions together to retrieve
                detailed information about an error.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRY

    SELECT 10 / 0;

END TRY
BEGIN CATCH

    SELECT
        ERROR_NUMBER() AS ErrorNumber,
        ERROR_MESSAGE() AS ErrorMessage,
        ERROR_SEVERITY() AS ErrorSeverity,
        ERROR_STATE() AS ErrorState,
        ERROR_PROCEDURE() AS ErrorProcedure,
        ERROR_LINE() AS ErrorLine;

END CATCH;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         12. THROW
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                12. THROW Statement
            </h2>

            <p>
                The <strong>THROW</strong> statement can be used to
                generate an exception or re-throw an existing error.
            </p>

            <p>
                It is commonly used inside a CATCH block when the
                application needs to pass the error to another layer.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRY

    SELECT 10 / 0;

END TRY
BEGIN CATCH

    THROW;

END CATCH;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         13. CUSTOM THROW ERROR
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                13. Creating a Custom Error using THROW
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>THROW 50001,
      'Employee salary cannot be negative.',
      1;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3 mb-0">

                User-defined error numbers used with THROW should be
                greater than or equal to <strong>50000</strong>.

            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         14. RAISERROR
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                14. RAISERROR
            </h2>

            <p>
                <strong>RAISERROR</strong> can be used to generate a
                custom error message.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>RAISERROR(
    'Something went wrong.',
    16,
    1
);</code></pre>

            <div class="alert alert-warning mt-3 mb-0">

                <strong>Note:</strong>
                For newer SQL Server code, <strong>THROW</strong> is
                generally preferred for raising and re-throwing errors.

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         15. ERROR HANDLING WITH TRANSACTION
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                15. Error Handling with Transactions
            </h2>

            <p>
                TRY...CATCH is very important when working with
                database transactions.
            </p>

            <p>
                If an error occurs after a transaction starts,
                the transaction can be rolled back.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRY

    BEGIN TRANSACTION;

    UPDATE Employees
    SET Salary = Salary + 1000
    WHERE EmployeeId = 1;

    COMMIT TRANSACTION;

END TRY
BEGIN CATCH

    IF @@TRANCOUNT &gt; 0
        ROLLBACK TRANSACTION;

    SELECT ERROR_MESSAGE() AS ErrorMessage;

END CATCH;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         16. PRACTICAL EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                16. Practical Error Handling Example
            </h2>

            <p>
                The following example demonstrates a complete error
                handling pattern.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>BEGIN TRY

    BEGIN TRANSACTION;

    UPDATE Employees
    SET Salary = Salary + 5000
    WHERE EmployeeId = 101;

    IF @@ROWCOUNT = 0
    BEGIN
        THROW 50001,
              'Employee was not found.',
              1;
    END;

    COMMIT TRANSACTION;

END TRY
BEGIN CATCH

    IF @@TRANCOUNT &gt; 0
        ROLLBACK TRANSACTION;

    SELECT
        ERROR_NUMBER() AS ErrorNumber,
        ERROR_MESSAGE() AS ErrorMessage,
        ERROR_LINE() AS ErrorLine;

END CATCH;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         17. IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-warning mb-4">

        <h3 class="fw-bold">
            Important Points
        </h3>

        <ul class="mb-0">

            <li>
                Use <strong>TRY...CATCH</strong> to handle runtime errors.
            </li>

            <li>
                Use <strong>ERROR_MESSAGE()</strong> to get the error message.
            </li>

            <li>
                Use <strong>ERROR_NUMBER()</strong> to get the error number.
            </li>

            <li>
                Use <strong>ERROR_LINE()</strong> to identify the error line.
            </li>

            <li>
                Use <strong>ERROR_PROCEDURE()</strong> to identify the
                procedure or trigger.
            </li>

            <li>
                Use <strong>THROW</strong> to generate or re-throw errors.
            </li>

            <li>
                When using transactions, check
                <strong>@@TRANCOUNT</strong> before rolling back.
            </li>

            <li>
                Proper error handling helps maintain database consistency.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="p-4 mb-4 rounded"
         style="background-color:#cfe2ff;">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Summary
        </h2>

        <p>
            Error Handling provides a structured way to manage errors
            in SQL Server.
        </p>

        <p>
            The <strong>TRY...CATCH</strong> mechanism allows SQL Server
            applications to detect and handle runtime errors.
        </p>

        <p class="mb-0">
            Functions such as
            <strong>ERROR_NUMBER()</strong>,
            <strong>ERROR_MESSAGE()</strong>,
            <strong>ERROR_SEVERITY()</strong>,
            <strong>ERROR_STATE()</strong>,
            <strong>ERROR_LINE()</strong> and
            <strong>ERROR_PROCEDURE()</strong>
            provide useful error information.
        </p>

    </div>


